[s0l0!-header][h1]ES6 Promises[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A [b]Promise[/b] is a better way for asynchronous programming when compared to the common way of using a [b]setTimeout[/b]() type of method.

[s0l0!-header][b]Consider this example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1614"]setTimeout(function() {
  console.log("Work 1");
  setTimeout(function() {
    console.log("Work 2");
  }, 1000);
}, 1000);
console.log("End");[/code]
It prints "End", "Work 1" and "Work 2" in that order (the work is done asynchronously). But if there are more events like this, the code becomes very complex.

ES6 comes to the rescue in such situations. A [b]promise [/b]can be created as follows: [code format="js" ]new [b]Promise[/b](function(resolve, reject) {
    // Work
    if (success)
        resolve(result);
    else
        reject(Error("failure"));
}); [/code]
Here, [b]resolve [/b]is the method for success and [b]reject [/b]is the method for failure.

If a method returns a promise, its calls should use the [b]then [/b]method which takes two methods as input; one for success and the other for the failure.

[s0l0!-header][b]For Example: [/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="js" codeId="1615"]function asyncFunc(work) {
  return new [b]Promise[/b](function(resolve, reject) {
    if (work === "")
      reject(Error("Nothing"));
    setTimeout(function() {
      resolve(work);
    }, 1000);
  });
}

asyncFunc("Work 1") // Task 1
.[b]then[/b](function(result) {
  console.log(result);
  return asyncFunc("Work 2"); // Task 2
}, function(error) {
  console.log(error);
})
.[b]then[/b](function(result) {
  console.log(result);
}, function(error) {
  console.log(error);
});
console.log("End");[/code]
It also prints "End", "Work 1" and "Work 2" (the work is done asynchronously). But, this is clearly more readable than the previous example and in more complex situations it is easier to work with.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

