[s0l0!-header][h1]Iterators & Generators[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Symbol.iterator[/b] is the default iterator for an object. The for...of loops are based on this type of iterator.

In the example below, we will see how we should implement it and how [b]generator functions [/b]are used.
[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1193"]let myIterableObj = { 
  [[b]Symbol.iterator[/b]] : [b]function*[/b] () {
    yield 1; yield 2; yield 3;
...
console.log([[b]...myIterableObj[/b]]);[/code]
First, we create an object, and use the [b]Symbol.iterator[/b] and [b]generator function[/b] to fill it with some values.

In the second line of the code, we use a [b]*[/b] with the [b]function[/b] keyword. It's called a [b]generator function (or gen function)[/b].

For example, here is a simple case of how [b]gen functions[/b] can be useful: [code format="js" codeId="1194"][b]function*[/b] idMaker() {
  let index = 0;
  while (index < 5)
    [b]yield[/b] index++;
}
var gen = idMaker();
console.log([b]gen.next().value[/b]);[/code]
We can exit and re-enter generator functions later. Their variable bindings (context) will be saved across re-entrances. They are a very powerful tool for asynchronous programming, especially when combined with Promises. They can also be useful for creating loops with special requirements.

We can nest [b]generator functions[/b] inside each other to create more complex structures and pass them arguments while we are calling them.
The example below will show a useful case of how we can use [b]generator functions[/b] and [b]Symbol.iterators[/b] together.
[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1195"]const arr = ['0', '1', '4', 'a', '9', 'c', '16'];
const my_obj = {
  [[b]Symbol.iterator[/b]]: [b]function*[/b]() {
    for(let index of arr) {
      [b]yield[/b] `${index}`;
    }
  }
};
const all = [[b]...my_obj[/b]]
  .[b]map[/b](i => parseInt(i, 10))
  .[b]map[/b](Math.sqrt)
  .[b]filter[/b]((i) => i < 5)
  .[b]reduce[/b]((i, d) => i + d);
console.log(all);[/code]
We create an object of 7 elements by using [b]Symbol.iterator[/b] and [b]generator functions[/b]. In the second part, we assign our object to a constant [b]all[/b]. At the end, we print its value.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

